#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# threaded

#% text
# `threaded(v)` marks `v` as an operand to be spread over the trailing levels of the arrays it is combined with; `threaded(v, n)` spreads it from level `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# By itself `threaded(v)` is inert: it stands for `v`, placed along some levels of the other arguments by `elementwise`.

#% item
# `threaded(v)` lines the dimensions of `v` up with the last levels of the other arguments; `threaded(v, n)` with `n > 0` puts the first dimension of `v` at level `n`, and with `n < 0` at level `r + n + 1`, where `r` is the rank of the array it is combined with.

#% item
# A threaded value whose dimensions do not fit gives `failure(shape_mismatch)`, one placed too deep `failure(threaded_level, n)`.

#% item
# Combining only threaded values (and scalars) gives a threaded result.

#% item
# Arithmetic and every listable function spread a threaded operand the same way: `[[1, 2], [3, 4]] + threaded([x, y])` is `[[1 + x, 2 + y], [3 + x, 4 + y]]`, and `2*threaded([-1, 1])` is `threaded([-2, 2])`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A row spread over every row of a matrix:

#% cell
elementwise(add)([[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]], threaded([x, y]))
#% output
#= [[1 + x, 2 + y], [3 + x, 4 + y], [5 + x, 6 + y]]

#% text
# Spread from the first level, the entries then go down the columns:

#% cell
elementwise(add)([[1, 2], [3, 4]], threaded([x, y], 1))
#% output
#= [[1 + x, 2 + x], [3 + y, 4 + y]]

#% text
# Only threaded values give a threaded value:

#% cell
elementwise(mul)(2, threaded([-1, 1]))
#% output
#= threaded([-2, 2])

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A matrix spread over the last two levels of an array of rank 3:

#% cell
elementwise(add)(array_reshape(range(8), [2, 2, 2]), threaded([[10, 20], [30, 40]]))
#% output
#= [[[11, 22], [33, 44]], [[15, 26], [37, 48]]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Threaded operands combine in the arithmetic: the sum of two is threaded, a threaded vector added to a matrix goes along the rows, and a threaded matrix of the same shape pairs entry by entry:

#% cell
[threaded([1, 2]) + threaded([10, 20]), [[1, 2], [3, 4]] + threaded([10, 20]), [[1, 2], [3, 4]] + threaded([[10, 20], [30, 40]])]
#% output
#= [threaded([11, 22]), [[11, 22], [13, 24]], [[11, 22], [33, 44]]]

#% text
# A negative level counts from the last level, so level `-1` is the default and `-2` of a matrix is the first level:

#% cell
[elementwise(add)([[1, 2], [3, 4]], threaded([10, 20], -1)), elementwise(add)([[1, 2], [3, 4]], threaded([10, 20], -2))]
#% output
#= [[[11, 22], [13, 24]], [[11, 12], [23, 24]]]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Dimensions that do not fit:

#% cell
elementwise(add)([[1, 2], [3, 4]], threaded([x, y, z]))
#% output
#! failure(shape_mismatch)

#% text
# A level beyond the rank of the array is an error:

#% cell
elementwise(add)([[1, 2], [3, 4]], threaded([10, 20], 5))
#% output
#! failure(threaded_level, 5)

#% text
# Alone, a threaded value stays threaded, and a function of it is not evaluated:

#% cell
[sin(threaded([0, pi/2])), threaded(5)]
#% output
#= [sin(threaded([0, (1/2)*pi])), threaded(5)]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `elementwise`
